That's the trick.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.850
FRACESC CAMPOY: Oh.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:11.570
Yeah, myself.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:15.140
So I was a Google Site
Reliability Engineer

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.730
at some point in my career.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:20.660
And then from Google, went
straight to SoundCloud.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.610
From Zurich to Berlin.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.460
And at SoundCloud, we
started building Prometheus.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:29.210
So that's what I've been doing
for roughly the past five years

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:30.080
or so.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.350
I left SoundCloud roughly
1 and 1/2 years back,

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:37.790
and then started doing just
freelancing around Prometheus.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.400
Prometheus is a
monitoring system that

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.230
is really independent by now.

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:45.800
It's part of the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation,

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.110
which in turn is part
of the Linux Foundation.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:50.950
Kubernetes was the
first project in there,

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:52.070
and we are the second.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:55.160
So it means there's
no one company that

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.080
controls the project.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.290
There are companies that
offer services around it,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.490
but they are not the
main Prometheus company.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:05.000
And SoundCloud doesn't even
care that much about Prometheus

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.070
anymore, although
it was born there.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.470
MARK MANDEL: OK, so let
us talk about Prometheus.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.120
At a high level, what is it?

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:11.810
What does it do?

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.340
What's its purpose?

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.560
JULIUS VOLZ: Right.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:20.330
So Prometheus is a systems
monitoring tool kit slash

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.590
ecosystem.

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.930
Some years in the
past, there were

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:28.340
systems like Nagios, Graphite,
Ganglia, StatsD, Munin,

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:29.000
and so on.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.410
Some for collecting time series.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:34.160
Some for running checks to
actually send alerts to people.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.880
And of course, these
tools still exist,

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:37.310
and they have their uses.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.110
But they were created
in a world that

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:43.190
was way more static than
the infrastructure worlds

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:46.700
that we have nowadays,
where you have clusters,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.710
like Kubernetes,
cluster managers that

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:52.080
schedule instances and plods
dynamically, moving around

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.350
a scaling up, scaling down.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.410
And so, Prometheus is
a monitoring system

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.440
that was specifically built
for this kind of dynamic cloud

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:03.050
environment, where these
things are shifting around

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:06.350
all the time, and you
still want to try and make

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.930
sense of things.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.340
And we started building
Prometheus really because--

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:12.840
so this was back in 2012.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:15.840
Matt Proud and myself
joining SoundCloud,

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:20.790
both coming from Google,
having seen how you can

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:22.730
monitor dynamic environments.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.659
And then being thrown
into a startup,

00:08:25.659 --> 00:08:27.200
basically, SoundCloud--
which already

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.460
had a dynamic environment.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:31.430
They had a dynamic
cluster scheduler,

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.830
but they still had the old
kind of monitoring tools.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:38.539
And they were OK, but they
weren't really cutting it

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:40.440
anymore for this
kind of scenario.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.980
So it was really
difficult to find out,

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.750
for example, if there
was a latency spike.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:45.890
Was it only one instance?

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.210
Was it multiple?

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:48.800
Which instance was it?

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.590
The dimensionality of Graphite
wasn't really sufficient.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:58.960
The alerting expressiveness,
and data model of Nagios,

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.040
and the lack of any history
in Nagios to alert on

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:04.670
was another problem.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:08.660
And so, Prometheus was really
inspired by Google's approach

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.190
to monitoring.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:14.110
And takes an approach of
let's collect everything

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.550
that we care about
in our infrastructure

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:20.390
as a set of metrics, as a
set of time series first.

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:25.370
And then base any dashboards
and alerts on these time series.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:30.140
And to allow you to make
that powerful enough,

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:33.770
we also basically
added a query language

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.080
to Prometheus that
is quite flexible,

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.200
that allows you to work with
the data that you collected,

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.510
and formulate really powerful
expressions against the data.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.060
To slice and dice the
data, aggregate up,

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:50.480
drill down, depending on
what you need in that moment.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:51.230
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.990
So to give some more context,
and maybe for those people who

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.420
aren't as familiar for
monitoring and stuff,

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.030
could you give an
example of step-by-step

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:00.997
through what you
would be setting up,

00:10:00.997 --> 00:10:03.080
and why you'd be setting
it up, and how would that

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:04.670
work with Prometheus?

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:07.180
JULIUS VOLZ: Yeah, there's
so many different ways

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:08.940
I could set things up.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:13.660
Prometheus is really kind of a
Swiss army knife of monitoring.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:15.520
Let's start with a
simple case of we

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:19.000
just want to gather host
metrics or note metrics.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.210
Then we could install
a little component,

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:26.110
called the Note Exporter, which
is a agent on every machine.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.140
And it will basically
fetch metrics

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:33.070
about the machine itself from
the proc and the sysfs file

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:34.090
systems.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:37.000
And it has a little
HTTP endpoint

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:39.670
over which Prometheus,
the server,

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:43.060
can scrape metrics
from that host.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:44.980
So you set up a
Prometheus server.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.560
And you tell it, here
are all of my hosts

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.660
that I care about that
I want to monitor.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:51.910
And it will then go out
to these Note Exporters

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:58.750
and will pull in a dimensional
data model all these metrics.

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.000
They will be tasked with
something we called labels.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:03.370
They're key value
pairs on every metric

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:06.490
that tell you what service
the metrics came from, what

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:08.320
exact instance in
the service, and then

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.600
what other kind of
subdimensions they

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:15.080
pertain to, like the exact CPU
core for CPU usage or so on.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:16.660
MARK MANDEL: And
then from there, you

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:17.550
can do stuff with it?

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:17.860
JULIUS VOLZ: Right.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.090
So Prometheus stores that.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:20.980
Prometheus is a
pull based system.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.110
It pulls those
metrics from things

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:24.850
you care about,
like Note Exporters,

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.160
but also your services
or other things.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.220
It stores them as
time series on disk.

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:33.831
And then you can use that
in three different ways

00:11:33.831 --> 00:11:34.330
I would say.

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:37.750
Either for ad-hoc debugging,
you go to Prometheus itself.

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:42.820
It has a built-in UI for trying
out some Prometheus query

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:45.460
language, Prom QL expressions,
where you can really

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:48.690
inspect the current state
of all your infrastructure.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:50.770
And once you have
expressions there

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:53.320
that you might want to
dashboard, you can use a tool,

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:57.370
like Grafana to talk to
Prometheus's HTTP API

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:00.160
to run the exact
same queries and have

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.660
nice persistent dashboards that
you can share with your team,

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:06.530
throw on the wall, and so on.

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:08.140
And then the third
use case would

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:12.010
be alerting, where you
can reuse the same query

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:13.750
language for
actually formulating

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:17.717
really precise alerts against
the data you collected.

00:12:17.717 --> 00:12:20.300
MARK MANDEL: So I might be able
to set something up that says,

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.334
if my CPU's been at
95% for five minutes,

00:12:23.334 --> 00:12:25.000
then send me an alert,
because something

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.840
is going wrong on the server
or something like that?

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:27.715
JULIUS VOLZ: Exactly.

00:12:27.715 --> 00:12:28.330
Yep.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:32.260
And you can do things that are
not even strictly threshold

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:32.950
related.

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:35.740
So for example, in Nagios, you
can only really say something

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:38.680
like, if this check script
fails three times in a row,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:40.630
then alert me.

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:42.730
And you don't really
collect history first

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:45.460
that you can then do more
analysis over, right?

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:48.280
It doesn't collect
any time series data.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:51.280
With Prometheus, you collect
basically some amount

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:52.280
of time series data.

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:55.350
And you can do some more
time-based analysis on it.

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:58.840
For example, you can say,
based on the last four

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:03.280
hours of the disk usage,
tell me whether my disk will

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.100
be full in four hours or
so, and then alert on that.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:08.210
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I see.

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:10.250
So sort of predictive
analysis type things?

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.900
JULIUS VOLZ: So there's limited
forms of predictive analysis.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:17.440
There's no automatic anomaly
detection, no kind of magic

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:18.010
there.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:20.230
But it's explicit rules,
explicit functions

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:22.180
that allow you to go quite far.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:22.990
FRACESC CAMPOY: So
that sounds really

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:25.240
interesting from the point of
view of like older features

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:25.870
that it offers.

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:28.970
But I'm curious about
how it actually runs.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:31.960
So you mentioned that
it is pull-based system.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:33.670
So you have a server
that is pulling

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:35.045
every single one of the jobs.

00:13:35.045 --> 00:13:36.670
Does that mean that
you need to install

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:39.160
an agent in every
single one of your jobs.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:40.899
What does that look like?

00:13:40.899 --> 00:13:42.440
JULIUS VOLZ: So it
does mean that you

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:46.240
do have to have HTTP end
points that you can scrape.

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:49.810
If it's a piece of software that
you cannot instrument directly,

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:52.900
we typically have these things
called Exporters that are

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:55.660
little bridges that sit between
Prometheus and the thing you

00:13:55.660 --> 00:14:00.051
care about, and translate the
metrics into the Prometheus

00:14:00.051 --> 00:14:00.550
format.

00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:03.020
So I already mentioned
the Note Exporter,

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:05.500
which translates sys
and procfs metrics

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:07.200
into Prometheus metrics.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:10.090
Then we have, for example,
a MySQL daemon exporter

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:13.450
that, in the back end, talks
actually SQL queries to a MySQL

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:16.600
daemon, and gets internal
stats about that server,

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.000
and translates that
to Prometheus metrics.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:21.430
And we have by now,
I think over 100

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:25.660
of these integrations in both
in our official organization

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:27.556
and provided by the community.

00:14:27.556 --> 00:14:28.930
And they're pretty
easy to write.

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:30.680
They're basically just
a small translation

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.520
layers between metric systems.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.280
And then the golden
thing, what we really

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:40.330
recommend is, if you
have services yourselves,

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:43.090
that you build yourself where
you control the code, really

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:46.750
instrument them directly using
one of the client libraries

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:49.970
that we provide, where
you get API object,

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:54.040
such as a counter, a gauge,
histogram, or a summary,

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:57.807
that allow you to track various
states in your own servers,

00:14:57.807 --> 00:14:59.890
and exactly the ones that
you actually care about.

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:03.190
So really nice white box
instrumentation of your code.

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:07.529
And that's really how you can
also get the best alerting.

00:15:07.529 --> 00:15:09.320
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
curious about something

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:10.111
that you mentioned.

00:15:10.111 --> 00:15:11.920
So basically, the
way I understand it,

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:13.795
Prometheus goes in outs,
and goes and scrapes

00:15:13.795 --> 00:15:15.794
these HTTP end point on
all the different things

00:15:15.794 --> 00:15:17.140
that you have running outside.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.474
But you're also talking about
very dynamic environments.

00:15:19.474 --> 00:15:21.390
So something like
Kubernetes, where containers

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:22.342
are going up and down.

00:15:22.342 --> 00:15:24.550
Or possibly, environments
where VMs are being created

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.080
and destroyed constantly.

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.570
How does Prometheus know
when those things are

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.570
spun up or been destroyed so it
knows to go and grab that data

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:32.800
from where it needs to?

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:35.500
JULIUS VOLZ: Thank you
for the great question.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:38.440
This is one of the key
parts of Prometheus

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:40.120
that make it
special, I would say.

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:43.270
It integrates with
service discovery

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:46.690
and with whatever kind
of service discovery

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:50.680
your infrastructure has.

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:53.110
Out of the box, we
currently support roughly 10

00:15:53.110 --> 00:15:54.470
different mechanisms.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:57.790
So these can be ones
where you discover AWS,

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:01.980
or like EC2, or Google
Compute Engine instances,

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.150
or so on a host level.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:07.060
But we also integrate with
some of the cluster schedulers,

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:10.840
like Kubernetes, where we talk
to the Kubernetes API server

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:14.400
to provide us the list of
all the service's end points,

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:17.470
and pods, and so running
on that Kubernetes cluster.

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:22.210
And the nice thing is this kind
of serves multiple purposes.

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:26.140
On one hand, we know then where
we need to go to get metrics.

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:28.970
So you know the actual
HTTP end points.

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:32.210
On the other hand, we know what
should be there in the world.

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:35.170
And if we cannot pull from
it, we know that it's done.

00:16:35.170 --> 00:16:37.600
You can base simple health
alerting on this already

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:38.336
on the signal.

00:16:38.336 --> 00:16:40.210
And the third thing
which is just really cool

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:43.450
is if you're using a good
service discovery mechanism,

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:45.320
like the one in
Kubernetes for example,

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:48.430
you get a lot of metadata
about the things you discover.

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:50.890
So you don't only
know that there

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:53.800
is an HTTP end point that
you should pull from,

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:56.560
but you also get all the
Kubernetes annotations,

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.650
and labels, and other
metadata that you can then

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:02.530
choose to either map
into your time series

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:05.079
or also make decisions on.

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:07.990
You can have an annotation
that says whether to scrape

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:09.829
a target at all or not.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:10.410
Yeah.

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.410
But just mapping the metadata
into your time series

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.480
is very nice, because then
you can see this engine x

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:19.630
over there is in environment
equals production instance,

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:20.390
for example.

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:23.770
And then you can use that in
your queries, in your alerts.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:26.290
So yeah, the service
discovery integration

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:29.950
is something that is pretty
new in monitoring systems,

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:32.020
and has been ignored
in the static world.

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:33.760
I mean, in the static
world, you kind

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:36.340
of just configure
what you expect.

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:39.340
Or often with
push-based systems,

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:41.920
where the instrument
and services themselves

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:45.080
push metrics to the
monitoring system,

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.580
the question of what even
should be there in the world

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:50.020
has often just been ignored.

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:52.810
Like if an instance or a
host never reports, then

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:54.580
you might never know
that it's missing.

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:56.746
FRACESC CAMPOY: So I have
a couple of questions now.

00:17:56.746 --> 00:18:00.580
You keep on saying this word
"time series" over and over.

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:02.020
And I'm curious about it.

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:05.530
On one side, you say that
you exposed that time series,

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:08.350
that data from every
single one of the elements

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:09.580
that you're monitoring.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:11.410
What is the format for that?

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:14.142
Is that like JSON,
Particle buffers,

00:18:14.142 --> 00:18:15.100
how do you expose that?

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:16.683
Because I guess that
is very important

00:18:16.683 --> 00:18:18.110
that that's efficient, right?

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:19.210
JULIUS VOLZ: Yep.

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:21.460
We had JSON at the very
beginning of Prometheus,

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:23.200
and then dumped
that at some point

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:26.230
for, not only, but also
efficiency reasons.

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:28.780
Now we have two
formats, a text based

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:31.450
one, which is very simple to
construct, and nice to read,

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:33.770
and very efficient to parse.

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:41.050
Where basically, the format
contains one time series sample

00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:41.980
per line.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:43.570
And we also have
a protocol buffer

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:47.680
based format, which can be even
more efficient and a bit more

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:50.320
robust, and then can then
easily be implemented

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.270
by any protocol buffer parser.

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:56.440
But in reality, there are not
actually many implementations

00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:58.739
that need to parse the
protocol, because it's mainly

00:18:58.739 --> 00:19:00.280
the Prometheus server
doing that job.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:03.130
So we really want to
optimize the easy production,

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:05.620
so that you can, even
from a shell script,

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:08.900
really easily just spit
out a metric, and so on.

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:11.590
So the text format seems
to be the one that actually

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.340
is getting the most traction.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:14.890
And some client
libraries don't even

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:18.430
bother implementing the protocol
buffer format at the moment.

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:20.680
FRACESC CAMPOY: So you're
getting all that information

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:22.210
from a lot of different agents.

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:23.626
And I'm assuming
that that's going

00:19:23.626 --> 00:19:25.330
to be a decent amount
of data that you

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.514
want to store somewhere.

00:19:26.514 --> 00:19:27.430
Where do you store it?

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:29.097
What is the database
behind all of this?

00:19:29.097 --> 00:19:29.888
JULIUS VOLZ: Right.

00:19:29.888 --> 00:19:30.405
Yeah.

00:19:30.405 --> 00:19:33.074
Prometheus's data is
highly dimensional.

00:19:33.074 --> 00:19:34.990
You're not only getting
from a lot of sources,

00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:37.450
but those sources
might sub-differentiate

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:40.585
their own data up by a lot
of labels and dimensions.

00:19:40.585 --> 00:19:44.260
Like what CPU did the
CPU usage happen in?

00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:45.820
You know, what
mode did it happen?

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:47.200
And so on and so on.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:50.500
Which file system
did some file system

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:53.320
fullness metric pertain to?

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:56.000
And we store all the data
currently on local disk.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:58.960
Prometheus itself only knows
how to store local disk.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:02.330
This is intentional, because
it keeps things simple.

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:04.290
You don't have to run
a clustered distributed

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:07.680
system, which might be the
first one that goes down

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:09.540
in the face of a
network petition

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:11.190
or some other
trouble when you need

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:13.680
your monitoring system the most.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.140
The next question then
is usually, but how

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:18.330
do you keep that
highly available?

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:20.370
The way you do that
is by just running

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:22.470
two identically
configured Prometheus

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:26.520
servers that know exactly
nothing about each other.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:29.160
They're configured to scrape
exactly the same targets.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:32.640
There's no history or any kind
of historical state in targets.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.700
The targets really just know
the last value of any metric.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:37.770
So what the current value.

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:39.270
So you can scrape
them in parallel

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:42.630
by as many Prometheus's as you
want, and it still works out

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:43.930
fine.

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:45.600
And then both of
these servers can

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.510
calculate alerting
rules by themselves,

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:51.540
and dispatch alerts
to a component also

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:54.360
of the Prometheus ecosystem,
called the Alert Manager.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:57.480
And that then actually
de-duplicates these alerts,

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:00.450
and routes them to recipients.

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:03.810
So that does mean that
the local storage isn't

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:05.820
infinitely scalable or durable.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.800
If you lose a node, it's gone.

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:10.080
And you know, if you
lose two nodes and turn,

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:13.450
and they come back up, they
won't backfill from the other.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:16.560
So for people who do
want to store data

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:19.980
for a really long time with
a guarantee of not losing it,

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:23.760
we recently added a remote,
write, and read back

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:27.840
integration protocol that
people could integrate with.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:30.320
Where Prometheus
basically sends any sample

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:34.410
that it stores also to a remote
URL that you can specify.

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:38.250
And actually, if your
bridge implements it,

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.200
you can also then
read back the data.

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:44.580
So for example, right now
we have an experimental toy

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.820
bridge, which knows how to write
data into InfluxDB, which you

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:51.100
can get in a clustered version
in a commercial version,

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:52.030
at least.

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:55.710
And then also read it back again
through PromQL in Prometheus,

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:57.770
but coming from Influx TB.

00:21:57.770 --> 00:22:01.480
So now you can nuke your local
database, and still get data.

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:05.430
But we really wanted to decouple
the actual really core alerting

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:07.914
functionality from that
remote storage aspect.

00:22:07.914 --> 00:22:09.330
FRACESC CAMPOY:
This really sounds

00:22:09.330 --> 00:22:12.390
like a good match for Bigtable.

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:13.440
JULIUS VOLZ: Yep.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:14.670
FRACESC CAMPOY: I'd like
to see if someone could

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:15.720
implement something like that.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:16.440
That'd be cool.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:18.600
JULIUS VOLZ: Well, this
is happening already.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:22.320
So there is also
cortex from Weaveworks,

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:25.860
which is both an open source
project and a hosted service

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.860
by them, which I also
was involved in building.

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:32.490
And this is a
reimplementation of Prometheus

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:35.670
based on currently AWS.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:38.610
But another company is
working on porting it over

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:40.440
to Google Cloud.

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.540
Also Bigtable there, by the way.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:46.560
And this is multitenant
horizontally scalable durable

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:48.030
forever, and so on.

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:50.970
And you basically configure
your Prometheus locally

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:54.520
to still discover targets
locally, pull targets locally,

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:56.940
but then send all the
collected samples over

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:57.990
to this cloud service.

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:01.540
Or you could run this
yourself as well.

00:23:01.540 --> 00:23:03.630
So yeah, that that
kind of already

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.437
exists or is being created.

00:23:05.437 --> 00:23:06.520
FRACESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:07.290
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:08.820
So I'm also just
curious, it looks

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:10.980
like there's a huge
community around Prometheus.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.110
Who's actually using it?

00:23:12.110 --> 00:23:13.320
Can you name any customers?

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:14.670
JULIUS VOLZ: Oh, I
have to be careful.

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:16.169
I would have to
look, and especially

00:23:16.169 --> 00:23:17.850
to see who I can name publicly.

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:21.060
There are a bunch of Fortune
500 companies, big ones.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:25.440
I think Walmart Labs has
been reasonably open.

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:26.370
New York Times.

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:28.710
Yeah, several big ones.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:30.360
Other ones are
thinking about it.

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.361
FRACESC CAMPOY: I guess,
SoundCloud is using it?

00:23:32.361 --> 00:23:34.860
JULIUS VOLZ: I don't want to
name any names that I shouldn't

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:35.850
name at the moment.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:38.308
So I would have to check the
list again to make sure that--

00:23:38.308 --> 00:23:40.320
MARK MANDEL: I'm just
looking at the web site.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:40.650
JULIUS VOLZ: Oh, yeah.

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:42.570
The website has
some that explicitly

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:44.660
asked us to add their logo.

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.770
But there is many more, and
really big ones as well.

00:23:47.770 --> 00:23:50.880
So I think one of
the nice things

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:54.480
for the adoption of
Prometheus that it also

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:56.610
works really nicely
together with Kubernetes.

00:23:56.610 --> 00:24:00.870
So Kubernetes is kind of really
inspired by Bork in Google.

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:05.220
There was a tool
inside Google, which

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:07.540
I think is mentioned in the
Google SRE e-book, called

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:10.500
"Borgmon," and that was a big
inspiration for Prometheus.

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:13.590
So there's some analogy
going on between Kubernetes

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.540
and Prometheus.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.710
They both use labels
in their data models.

00:24:18.710 --> 00:24:21.420
Kubernetes has native
Prometheus metrics.

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:25.565
Prometheus has native support
for Kubernetes's service

00:24:25.565 --> 00:24:26.520
discovery.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:28.230
So they kind of
go together well.

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:30.750
And I think a lot of
ex-Googlers and current Googlers

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:34.180
also noticed that, and picked
it up because of that reason.

00:24:34.180 --> 00:24:36.870
So I think that really
helps adoption, as well

00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:39.597
as being part of this Cloud
Native Computing Foundation.

00:24:39.597 --> 00:24:41.430
FRACESC CAMPOY: Also,
there's something else

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:44.130
that is in common between
Kubernetes and Prometheus,

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:44.850
right?

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:46.440
Which is they're
both written in Go?

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:48.315
JULIUS VOLZ: They're
both written in Go, yes.

00:24:48.315 --> 00:24:49.662
Single static binaries.

00:24:49.662 --> 00:24:50.370
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:55.540
Just because I'm super,
super curious as well.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:25:00.120
So if I want to install
Prometheus on Kubernetes,

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:02.400
should I install it
in the same cluster

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:04.150
that I'm already running
my other apps in?

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:05.890
Or should I run it in
a different cluster?

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:06.910
FRACESC CAMPOY: Who
monitors Prometheus?

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:07.660
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:09.370
Who monitors the
monitors, right?

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:10.120
JULIUS VOLZ: Yeah.

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:11.740
I think what people
normally do is

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:14.500
they do run their normal
Prometheus in the Kubernetes

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:16.090
cluster, because
that makes it easier

00:25:16.090 --> 00:25:17.815
to monitor targets in there.

00:25:17.815 --> 00:25:21.130
You automatically get
the necessary credentials

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:24.190
molded into your Kubernetes
pod to be able to talk

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:26.060
to the API server, and so on.

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:28.090
And you also have
immediate network access

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:32.170
to all the instances in the
pods that you need to pull from.

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:35.200
You can and should if you're
serious about monitoring,

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:38.410
then still have some external
meta-monitoring probably

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:41.440
that monitors your
Prometheus servers.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:43.640
Not only that they're
up, but the Prometheus

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:47.470
server itself also outputs
a lot of instrumentation

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:49.850
in the Prometheus format itself.

00:25:49.850 --> 00:25:53.090
So now you can really inspect
with some meta-monitoring

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:55.090
how all your Prometheus
servers are really

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:57.215
behaving, whether they're
overloaded, and so on.

00:25:57.215 --> 00:25:58.090
FRACESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:58.090 --> 00:26:00.280
I'm curious about--
since you were talking

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:02.350
about things are in common
in between Kubernetes

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:03.476
and Prometheus.

00:26:03.476 --> 00:26:05.100
One of the cool things
about Kubernetes

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:08.410
is that idea of having many
Kubernetes clusters working

00:26:08.410 --> 00:26:10.210
together as a federation.

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:12.550
Is there such a
thing for monitoring?

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:14.739
Or is it not necessary?

00:26:14.739 --> 00:26:16.780
JULIUS VOLZ: We have a
concept called federation.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:20.110
It's not exactly the same
as Kubernetes Federation.

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:23.350
The way it works is
that, let's say, you

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:26.620
have 10 data centers, or
10 Kubernetes clusters,

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:29.050
you might have for a
given service in there

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:33.790
or all the services run one
Prometheus server per cluster.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:37.810
That gives you a very detailed
local view of the universe.

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:42.370
But it might be that not all
the data of all 10 clusters

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:45.370
fits into one big global
Prometheus server.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.080
So you could have
still a federation

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:51.070
set up, where you have a
global Prometheus server, which

00:26:51.070 --> 00:26:55.060
scrapes federated data from
all the 10 subordinated

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:59.200
Prometheus's, but only gets kind
of roll-ups or aggregations,

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:02.800
where you maybe sum up over
all the instances of a given

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:07.100
service and don't have the
really detailed data anymore.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:10.480
So you can keep that global,
but not really detailed

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:12.160
level at the top.

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:14.370
And have local,
but very detailed

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:17.270
data at the bottom of
your federation tree.

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:21.440
And your federation tree could
even have multiple levels.

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:25.300
You can also use federation
to do it kind of sideways.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:28.300
You know, to pull data from a
Prometheus of a different team

00:27:28.300 --> 00:27:29.980
if you're also
interested in that.

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:32.300
Common use case would
be, for example,

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:36.070
if the team that runs
the Kubernetes cluster

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:37.480
has a Prometheus server.

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:39.310
And you have maybe
your own one that

00:27:39.310 --> 00:27:43.930
just monitors your direct
service metrics of your one

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:46.070
service running on Kubernetes.

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:50.980
You might be also interested in
externally observed container

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:54.550
resource usage metrics
for your services, right?

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:58.330
So you can pull them from the
other Prometheus server that

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:00.370
is already gathering
all that data

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:03.370
from the built-in
cAdvisors in the cubelets,

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:05.620
and then store that in
your Prometheus server.

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:07.990
Or you could also, if you
have the credentials, even

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:10.340
go out and scrape
that data yourself.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:13.460
But that's one
possibility as well.

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:17.239
So this allows you to scale
setups and be more flexible.

00:28:17.239 --> 00:28:19.780
FRACESC CAMPOY: So I guess we're
