00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:39.920
It's a happy world.

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:40.900
Cool.

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:44.049
So let's move on to our
question of the week.

00:29:44.049 --> 00:29:44.840
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:45.340
Yeah.

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:46.490
So this is a fun one.

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:50.200
So the idea here being is
that I built an application.

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:53.654
And maybe I forgot to add some
logging in some places that I

00:29:53.654 --> 00:29:54.820
probably should have, right?

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:55.700
Something went wrong.

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:56.950
Maybe I was just a bit rushed.

00:29:56.950 --> 00:29:58.000
Who knows, right?

00:29:58.000 --> 00:29:59.950
And something is
failing in production.

00:29:59.950 --> 00:30:02.020
And I'm thinking to
myself, ah, I really

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:04.090
wish I'd put some
logging in there.

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:06.940
But ideally, I mean, I
could just rewrite the code

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:08.280
and redeploy a new version.

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:09.020
That takes time.

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:09.770
That takes effort.

00:30:09.770 --> 00:30:11.450
I'd prefer not to do that.

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:14.410
Is there a way I can magically
just stick some log statements

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:16.150
inside my code and make it run?

00:30:16.150 --> 00:30:18.620
FRACESC CAMPOY: No, you should
have written better code.

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:20.370
It is your fault. And
you should feel bad.

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:21.536
MARK MANDEL: I feel bad now.

00:30:21.536 --> 00:30:22.304
I'm sorry.

00:30:22.304 --> 00:30:24.220
FRACESC CAMPOY: There
is actually a great way.

00:30:24.220 --> 00:30:26.100
We talked about this
a long time ago.

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:29.500
But from the of
Stackdriver debugger,

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:31.300
which is a really
awesome tool that

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:35.980
works on most environments in
most languages, except for Go

00:30:35.980 --> 00:30:36.594
on AppEngine.

00:30:36.594 --> 00:30:38.510
Other than that, pretty
much works everywhere.

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:42.010
So if you're using Node.js,
or Python, or Java, or Go,

00:30:42.010 --> 00:30:43.090
you should check it out.

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:44.830
Probably works.

00:30:44.830 --> 00:30:45.620
Probably works?

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:47.810
It works.

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:50.110
So what you can do
is you can go and set

00:30:50.110 --> 00:30:53.110
a breakpoint in the line
that you care about,

00:30:53.110 --> 00:30:54.455
you want to learn more about.

00:30:54.455 --> 00:30:56.860
What are the values of all
the variables at this point?

00:30:56.860 --> 00:30:59.495
If you did this for real, like
you actually put a break point,

00:30:59.495 --> 00:31:01.870
then you're actually stopping
your processing production,

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:03.100
and that's a big no.

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:04.340
So that's not what happens.

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:08.130
What happens is that we take
a snapshot of the whole call

00:31:08.130 --> 00:31:09.980
stack and the heap.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:12.460
So you can actually see
what are the variables, what

00:31:12.460 --> 00:31:14.890
are the values for every
single one of those variables.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:17.810
Now, the problem is that if you
do this, what you're getting

00:31:17.810 --> 00:31:20.590
is every single time
there is a new snap shot,

00:31:20.590 --> 00:31:21.610
you get to see it.

00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:24.144
And you inspect it
manually, right?

00:31:24.144 --> 00:31:26.060
And sometimes this is
not what you want to do.

00:31:26.060 --> 00:31:29.860
Sometimes what you want to do
is see a log of, not only ones,

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:33.850
but all of the times that
someone goes in an actual log.

00:31:33.850 --> 00:31:37.244
So then you can analyze it
with all the log analysis tools

00:31:37.244 --> 00:31:38.410
that you might already have.

00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:39.490
MARK MANDEL: And I
can see the history

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:40.930
and exactly what's going on.

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:43.096
FRACESC CAMPOY: This is
actually very useful, right?

00:31:43.096 --> 00:31:45.430
So literally everything
you want to do

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:49.270
is just add one line of logs,
and you can actually do that.

00:31:49.270 --> 00:31:51.236
It's what we call
debug log points.

00:31:51.236 --> 00:31:53.110
And it is as simple as
going to the debugger,

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:54.890
clicking on the line number.

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:57.070
And rather than adding a
breakpoint, what you are

00:31:57.070 --> 00:31:58.480
is a log point.

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:00.520
And then you can
write a little--

00:32:00.520 --> 00:32:04.030
it's basically a
line of code, that

00:32:04.030 --> 00:32:07.090
basically says if
some condition, then

00:32:07.090 --> 00:32:08.740
log some expression.

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:09.490
That it.

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:10.426
It's very simple.

00:32:10.426 --> 00:32:11.800
But that's exactly
what you want.

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:12.730
Super effective.

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:17.060
There's a really good video
by our dear colleague Aja

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:17.620
Hammerly.

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:19.670
It's one of these Cloud Minutes.

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:22.270
So in less than 60
seconds, you learn

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:24.989
how to use it, in
her case for Python,

00:32:24.989 --> 00:32:27.280
but it is pretty much the
same case for every language.

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:28.030
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:30.070
So now I can add logging
statements anywhere

